  "worked_solution": "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x(5-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=\\sqrt{5}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-{x}^{3})=5-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{1+{(5-3{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{26-30{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
